WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.334
-Welcome back.
-Thank you, buddy.

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:04.200
-I'm so happy you're here.
-Wow, thanks.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.400
-I have a lot
to talk to you about.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.467
-All right.
-First of all,

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:08.801
since "Better Call Saul"...
-Yeah.

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:11.868
-Okay, it has now ended.
It has come to an end.

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:13.167
-It did? Are you sure?
-Barely.

00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:15.033
It was so fresh.
I mean, no -- I know, it is.

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.901
-"They keep calling me back."

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.834
-But you did that character
for 13 years?

00:00:18.834 --> 00:00:23.434
-Yeah. I was supposed to do
three or four episodes.

00:00:23.434 --> 00:00:24.968
They asked. Yeah.
-No way.

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:27.834
-Yeah, and then it
turned into my whole life.

00:00:27.834 --> 00:00:29.234
So...
-[ Laughs ] -Yeah.

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:30.868
-And then --
I'm thankful for it.

00:00:30.868 --> 00:00:31.901
But it was --
-Are you happy with the way

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:33.434
everything wrapped up?
-Oh, very happy.

00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:35.334
And I don't know if everyone's
seen the finale,

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:38.834
but if you haven't yet, it --
it plays out really beautifully.

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:40.834
The writers
just did such a great job.

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:43.367
So, I'm -- I'll --
you know, always be thankful

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:44.934
for that role,
the role of a lifetime.

00:00:44.934 --> 00:00:48.067
I -- I -- the last season,
I was scared

00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:49.801
because we heard news
that you had a heart attack.

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:51.334
-Yeah, I did.
-You had a heart attack.

00:00:51.334 --> 00:00:54.100
-I sure did.
And, you know,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.367
it was a classic heart attack.

00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:57.634
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, wow!

00:00:57.634 --> 00:01:00.901
-I said, "Gimme the top --
the best one."

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:03.334
But it all --
it all played out fine

00:01:03.334 --> 00:01:05.200
because everyone was there
to save my life,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.701
and I had CPR done right away,
and I have two stents.

00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:10.033
-Someone did CPR on the set?
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:12.100
Yeah. The --
The ambulance didn't show up

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.734
for 25 minutes, so I --
I have two stents.

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.400
And I'm great.
I'm great.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.801
-I'm so happy, dude.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:22.367
I'm so happy.
I -- I -- 'cause --

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:25.667
I was like -- I got scared,
but I know you.

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:27.167
And I know you were
in great shape.

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:28.300
-I was.
-Because I saw "Nobody."

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.501
-Yeah. I did an action film
called "Nobody."

00:01:30.501 --> 00:01:32.167
-It's fantastic.
It's a great movie.

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:33.434
-It got me in --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:33.434 --> 00:01:36.267
Actually, you know,
I was in really good shape,

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:40.868
and that helped my heart
to survive that incident, so...

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:42.834
-Really?
-...exercise, and it'll

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:44.400
help you through everything.
-Yeah.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.667
-Yeah.
-I know you from "Mr. Show."

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.000
-As a comedy writer,
you know me.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.300
-I know you.
-Because that's what I did...

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.834
-"Saturday Night Live."
-...for most of my career.

00:01:51.834 --> 00:01:53.701
-Yeah.
-And then you started there

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:55.734
after I left.
-Yeah.

00:01:55.734 --> 00:01:58.234
I -- I once met you
on the street once in L.A.

00:01:58.234 --> 00:01:59.934
-And I do remember that.
-You do?

00:01:59.934 --> 00:02:01.501
-And it was outside
the Virgin Megastore.

00:02:01.501 --> 00:02:03.300
-Virgin Megastore.
-And you were either just about

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.167
to do the Boston movie or --
or had done it.

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:09.133
-No, right before.
I was brand-new

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:10.367
on "Saturday Night Live."
-Yeah, you were anxious.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:11.634
-And I was so nervous
to see you.

00:02:11.634 --> 00:02:13.033
-You were very nervous, yes.
-'Cause I was like,

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:14.534
"It's Bob Odenkirk."
-It was great talking to you.

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.701
-You were so nice to me.
I will never forget that

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:17.901
because when
you're a comedian --

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:20.367
-Well, as soon as you walked
away, I said...

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:21.567
-"I hate this guy."
-..."This [bleep] guy."

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.267
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.767
I said it to no one.
-"He talked my ear off."

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.367 --> 00:02:29.033
No, you were so nice to me.

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:31.033
And when you're first
starting out in comedy,

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:33.000
anyone who's nice to you,
just -- it takes just even

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.267
two minutes
of being nice to you.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:36.033
-Yeah, nothing -- I -- yes.
-I'll never forget.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:38.200
Robin Williams -- I remember
when David Cross and I

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.400
did "Comic Relief VIII,"
and we were doing "Mr. Show,"

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.434
and hardly anyone saw us when
that show was on -- or since.

00:02:45.434 --> 00:02:46.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:48.534
And -- And we did
"Comic Relief VIII,"

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:52.534
and we were so proud to be
invited and to be a part of it.

00:02:52.534 --> 00:02:56.234
And then only Robin
came to us and said,

00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:58.968
"You guys are really funny."
And he had seen "Mr. Show,"

00:02:58.968 --> 00:03:00.467
and it was so great.
-That's so great.

00:03:00.467 --> 00:03:02.167
-It meant so much to us.
-You don't forget that.

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:03.467
-No.
-It's like -- it's the best.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:05.434
-So, I'm looking at this list.
"Rolling Stone" released

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:08.300
this list of the 100 Greatest
TV Shows of All Time.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.667
-Oh.
-And it's always good.

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:10.934
It starts a little debate
and all this stuff.

00:03:10.934 --> 00:03:12.300
You know, "What is the best?"

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.834
You're on it three times, bud.
Three different shows.

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:16.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:18.901
Look at that.
That's not too shabby.

00:03:18.901 --> 00:03:22.234
"Mr. Show," "Better Call Saul,"
"Breaking Bad."

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:24.767
[ Applause ]

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:27.968
-And I don't know --
my publicist did not tell me

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:29.934
how much that cost, but...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.934 --> 00:03:32.534
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah.
But that's amazing.

00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:35.067
You had to go from a comedy --
a comedian -- a comedy writer...

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:36.534
-Yeah.
-...you know --

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:40.601
-Drama acting -- it's weird,
and somehow, I -- I don't know.

00:03:40.601 --> 00:03:41.801
Showbiz is weird.
You know that.

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:43.901
-Showbiz is weird, yeah.
But you're -- you --

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:45.234
you're a very talented guy.
-Oh, thank you, buddy.

00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:47.400
-You can do "Saul."
You just got honored with this.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.167
-Yeah.
-2023 Man of the Year...

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:50.934
-Look at this.
-...Hasty Pudding, Harvard.

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:52.467
-Harvard -- at Harvard.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:55.501
-Harvard's --
Harvard's Hasty Pudding.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.400
This is a big deal.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.567
-Okay.

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:01.434
So, I think that this means
I can tell people

00:04:01.434 --> 00:04:03.167
I went to Mr. Harvard's college.

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.267
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:06.234
Mr. Harvard.
You went to Harvard.

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:07.534
Yeah, exactly.
-I did.

00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:08.901
-What -- did it --
-It was great fun.

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:10.601
-I found your
college I.D. online.

00:04:10.601 --> 00:04:13.234
-Oh, yeah. And now
my real college experience.

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:14.367
Ready for this?
-"Look at this."

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:15.734
-Look out.
[ Laughs ]

00:04:15.734 --> 00:04:17.534
-What was this --
what was this dude all about?

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:19.033
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:21.067
What --
What was he all about?

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:23.067
-He was a good guy.
-Yeah.

00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:24.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.901 --> 00:04:27.567
-A little confused.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:29.834
Actually, what happened
there was --

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:31.467
I was very young
when I went to college.

00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:34.868
So, I'm 17, and this is
my second year of college.

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:36.968
And -- And I walk in
through the rain.

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:40.234
It was raining at SIU,
Southern Illinois University.

00:04:40.234 --> 00:04:42.400
It's -- picture day
was rainy day.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.701
And I --
-Drenched.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:47.234
-I wanted to be a writer.
I wanted to be a novelist,

00:04:47.234 --> 00:04:51.133
which is -- ties into this show
that I just made, "Lucky Hank,"

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.601
for AMC.
But I -- at that time,

00:04:53.601 --> 00:04:56.601
I wanted to be a serious writer.

00:04:56.601 --> 00:04:59.767
I love Jack Kerouac writing.
And I -- I read a lot.

00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:02.601
I read John Gardner
and literary criticism.

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:04.968
I really was serious about it.

00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:06.567
Except then, when I would
sit down to write,

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:08.234
I would just write
comedy sketches.

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:09.367
[ Laughs ]

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:11.567
I would write
parody commercials!

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:13.667
[ Laughter ]
Like, how is that --

00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:15.901
That's never gonna
get you to a novel.

00:05:15.901 --> 00:05:18.300
-No. But you -- you couldn't
stop writing funny bits.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.868
-But I just did it --
I just loved it.

00:05:19.868 --> 00:05:20.968
I -- I loved doing it.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:23.067
I love "Monty Python" so much.

00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:27.501
And after a few months and I had
a little radio show on the --

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:28.934
WIDB, the local radio station,
which no one listened to...

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:32.968
-Yeah.
-...which is great.

00:05:32.968 --> 00:05:36.534
When you're starting out,
do perform, write,

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:38.067
but make sure
there's no audience.

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:39.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:39.234 --> 00:05:40.901
-Yeah, 'cause then you --
that's good advice.

00:05:40.901 --> 00:05:43.400
-'Cause there's no record of it.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.534
It's so great
because I'm sure it was awful.

00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:48.133
But you know what?

00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:49.534
You know, you do that
for a couple years

00:05:49.534 --> 00:05:51.901
and you get good at something,
and eventually,

00:05:51.901 --> 00:05:54.701
I got to "SNL" when I was 25.
And --

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:57.534
And then I had to get good fast.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:58.667
-Yeah. You did.
And you got --

00:05:58.667 --> 00:06:00.267
So, let's talk about
"Lucky Hank."

00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:01.901
-"Lucky hank," the new show.
Yeah.

00:06:01.901 --> 00:06:03.567
-The other show is still fresh.

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:04.934
-Very fresh.
-You just finished that.

00:06:04.934 --> 00:06:06.601
And now you have this show.
-I know.

00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:08.534
-It premieres this Sunday.
You play Hank.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.501
What -- What is this?
-He's a college professor.

00:06:10.501 --> 00:06:13.367
He's a crabby old man.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:15.234
Well, look, I'm playing a guy
who's closer to my age,

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:16.400
which is kind of great.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.300
And he's funny.
He jokes constantly.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.200
And it's an amazing cast
of funny characters, actors,

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.968
great actors who play this,

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:28.767
you know, all these professors
in this English department.

00:06:28.767 --> 00:06:32.167
And it's really about,
they say, a midlife crisis.

00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:36.501
And it's just about people being
stupid and cruel to each other.

00:06:36.501 --> 00:06:37.667
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:39.934
Why not?
-Because -- Because in academia,

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:42.467
like any workplace situation,

00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:46.133
you prove your worth by getting
one over on the guy next to you

00:06:46.133 --> 00:06:47.734
or the lady next to you.
-Yeah.

00:06:47.734 --> 00:06:49.667
-And so that's what
these people are doing.

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:52.501
They're trajectorizing
and trying to make it.

00:06:52.501 --> 00:06:54.234
You know,
trying to be important.

00:06:54.234 --> 00:06:56.767
And so there's
a lot of comedy in it.

00:06:56.767 --> 00:06:59.100
Peter Farrelly did a wonderful
job directing our first episode.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.367
-Come on, Peter Farrelly.
-One of the greats.

00:07:01.367 --> 00:07:03.667
One of the greats.
-Oscar winner, by the way.

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:06.133
-Yeah, and I can't wait
for you to see it.

00:07:06.133 --> 00:07:09.701
If you're looking for some
comedy and drama, mixed 50/50,

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:11.400
not easy to do.
-Yeah.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:12.968
-That's what we shot for.
-I want to show a clip.

00:07:12.968 --> 00:07:14.601
Here's Bob Odenkirk
in "Lucky Hank."

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:16.334
Take a look at this.

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:18.934
-You're here.
You're here.

00:07:18.934 --> 00:07:21.567
The main piece of evidence
is that you are here.

00:07:21.567 --> 00:07:23.968
The fact that you're here
means you didn't try very hard

00:07:23.968 --> 00:07:25.834
in high school,
or for whatever reason,

00:07:25.834 --> 00:07:27.901
you showed very little promise.

00:07:27.901 --> 00:07:30.100
Did that sound harsh?
I'll tell you what.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.701
I'll smile
through the rest of this.

00:07:31.701 --> 00:07:34.400
You are here.
And even if you're presence

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.968
at this middling college
in this sad, forgotten town

00:07:36.968 --> 00:07:38.400
with some bizarre anomaly,

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.667
and you do have
the promise of genius,

00:07:40.667 --> 00:07:45.767
which I'll bet a kidney that you
don't, it will never surface.

00:07:45.767 --> 00:07:49.968
I am not a good enough writer
or writing teacher

00:07:49.968 --> 00:07:51.467
to bring it out of you.

00:07:51.467 --> 00:07:53.400
And how do I know that?
How?

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.133
Because I, too, am here!

00:07:58.133 --> 00:08:02.834
At Railton College,
mediocrity's capital.

00:08:02.834 --> 00:08:04.234
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:08:04.234 --> 00:08:09.200
-"I, too, am here.
So I can relate."

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.167
-Poor fella.
-That's so well done.

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:12.434
-Yeah.
-I don't know --

00:08:12.434 --> 00:08:14.667
I don't know how you do it.
But you just wrapped

00:08:14.667 --> 00:08:16.467
"Better Call Saul,"
and you already filmed

00:08:16.467 --> 00:08:18.534
a whole new other series.
-Whoa!

00:08:18.534 --> 00:08:21.501
One series?
I actually -- two.

00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:22.634
-What? I didn't --
-You know, there's anot--

00:08:22.634 --> 00:08:24.267
-I didn't know
you had another show.

00:08:24.267 --> 00:08:27.234
-Well, yeah.
This --

00:08:27.234 --> 00:08:30.367
I joined the cast
of "Blippi" this season.

00:08:30.367 --> 00:08:32.267
[ Laughter ]
-The -- The children's show,

00:08:32.267 --> 00:08:33.300
"Blippi"?
-Did they write that?

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.467
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:34.467 --> 00:08:37.901
-Well, I play Blippi's
evil nemesis, Gorpi.

00:08:37.901 --> 00:08:39.701
[ Laughter ]

00:08:39.701 --> 00:08:41.634
-I didn't even know Blippi
had a nemesis.

00:08:41.634 --> 00:08:44.501
I mean, what's going on --
on the show?

00:08:44.501 --> 00:08:46.200
-Just a lot of Blippi stuff,
you know.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:47.601
[ Laughter ]

00:08:47.601 --> 00:08:50.968
I mean, basically -- I guess
I should say spoiler alert.

00:08:50.968 --> 00:08:56.534
But my character, Gorpi,
stole Blippi's magic strawberry

00:08:56.534 --> 00:08:58.567
that teaches him the alphabet.
-Wow.

00:08:58.567 --> 00:09:01.367
-And now Blippi can't speak
because he forgot the alphabet.

00:09:01.367 --> 00:09:05.267
So that means he can't RSVP to
Mr. Toothbrush's cleanup party.

00:09:05.267 --> 00:09:06.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.534 --> 00:09:08.534
But what Blippi
doesn't know -- right? --

00:09:08.534 --> 00:09:10.601
-Uh-huh.
-Is that I've kidnapped his date

00:09:10.601 --> 00:09:12.868
to the cleanup party,
Mavis Manners.

00:09:12.868 --> 00:09:15.367
And this -- this is throwing
the entire world in turmoil

00:09:15.367 --> 00:09:16.534
because now --
[ Laughter ]

00:09:16.534 --> 00:09:18.100
And you guys are --
you already know this.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:21.167
But anyway, no one knows how to
say "please and thank you."

00:09:21.167 --> 00:09:24.000
Also, just, this entire season
is a dream

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.234
that the number eight is having.
-Well, that -- this is --

